(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608319602-one-namespace-two-files-one-of-them-dead")
  (:adr/title "1 \u3064\u306e namespace \u306b 2 \u3064\u306e\u30d5\u30a1\u30a4\u30eb\u3001\u7247\u65b9\u306f\u6b7b\u3093\u3067\u3044\u308b \u2014 .clj/.cljs \u304c .cljc \u3092\u96a0\u3059\u7d44\u3092\u5168 repo \u3067\u6570\u3048\u308b\u691c\u67fb\u3002src/ \u3060\u3051\u898b\u308b\u3068 4\u3001repo \u3092\u6b69\u304f\u3068 25")
  (:adr/status :accepted)
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/deciders "owner")
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-native"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608319601-the-jvm-free-project-linker-was-one-printer" "adr-2608136000-six-questions" "kotoba-native-ADR-0036" "aiueos-ADR-0129"))
  (:adr/body "## Decision\n\n`scripts/verify-namespace-twins.cljs` walks every repository under `orgs/` and\nreports namespaces that exist as both `.cljc` and a platform-specific sibling.\nSix pairs are declared intentional in the script, with the reason for each. The\nrest are reported as unexamined.\n\n## Why\n\nClojure resolves `.clj` before `.cljc` on the JVM and `.cljs` before `.cljc` on\nClojureScript. A namespace present as both therefore has one file that wins on\nthat platform and one that is never loaded there. Sometimes that is the point.\nUsually it is an accident, and the accident is silent: edits to the shadowed\nfile compile, pass review, and change nothing.\n\nkotoba-native's `kotoba.native.elf64` is why this exists. Its two files are a\ndeliberate twin \u2014 that repo's ADR-0036 states the rule, *the JVM file must not\nbe a weaker allowlist than the portable one* \u2014 and nothing measured the claim.\nThey drifted to 74 entries against 69, and because the JVM file is the one that\nruns, three aiueos kernel objects stopped building. They reported\n`:kotoba/internal-error`, \"internal compiler error\", which is what a reader\nwould call a compiler crash and was nothing of the kind.\n\n## The sibling axis, already recorded\n\nADR-2608120600 asks the same question one level out: two REPOSITORIES\npublishing the same namespace, where the classpath decides which wins. It\nmeasured 455 such namespaces, 224 of them divergent, 27 able to land on one\nclasspath, and it has its own detector,\n`scripts/verify-namespace-collisions.cljs`.\n\nSame family \u2014 something silently wins and the loser's edits do nothing \u2014\ndifferent resolver. This one is decided by the loader's extension priority\ninside a single repository, not by classpath order between repositories. The\ntwo scripts now point at each other.\n\nThat ADR's warning transfers directly and is the reason the table below\nseparates *declared intentional* from *unexamined* rather than from *broken*:\n**a content difference is not by itself a signal.** Most of the divergence it\nfound was scaffolding that is supposed to differ.\n\n## Where you look decides the answer\n\nScanning `src/` finds **4** pairs. Scanning the repositories finds **25**.\n\nThe first number is not wrong about `src/`. It is wrong as an answer to *how\nmany are there*, and nothing in its output says which question it answered.\nThat is the same defect this workspace keeps finding in its own checks, and it\nappeared here in the very sweep written to look for it \u2014 the first version of\nthis scan walked `src/` and reported four with no caveat.\n\nThe script now walks whole repositories, prints how many it walked, and refuses\nwith a distinct exit code when it walks none. `.claude/worktrees` is skipped:\na pair inside a transient worktree is the same pair already counted at its real\nhome, and counting it twice inflates the number in the direction that looks\nlike diligence.\n\n## Current state, measured 2026-08-31\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Repositories walked | 4,770 |\n| Twin namespaces | 25 |\n| Declared intentional, with reasons in the script | 6 |\n| Unexamined | 19 \u2014 of which 14 differ between the two files and 5 are byte-identical |\n\nThe six intentional ones are the two `kotoba-lang` conformance fixtures whose\nSUBJECT is exactly this resolution order (present in both the main repository\nand the hardening fork), `kotobase-engine-prolly`'s provider \u2014 a JVM\ncoordinator beside a ClojureScript/R2 coordinator, different implementations on\npurpose \u2014 and `elf64`, which is now kept identical by a parity test.\n\n## What this does NOT claim\n\n**The nineteen are unexamined, not defects.** No harm has been demonstrated for\nany of them. A byte-identical pair costs nothing today and is only a latent\nhazard: the next edit to the shadowed copy is the one that disappears. A\ndiffering pair may be a deliberate platform split that simply has not been\nwritten down.\n\nOnly `elf64` was traced from a twin to a consequence, and only because a\nseparate measurement went looking for why four objects would not compile.\n\n## The first one examined, and what examining it changed\n\n`cloud-itonami/mangaka`'s `clj/test/mangaka/core_test` was the pair that most\nlooked worth reading \u2014 a 1,318-line `.clj` shadowing a 2,050-line `.cljc`.\nExamined 2026-08-31:\n\n- The runner is JVM `cognitect.test-runner` (`clojure -M:test`), so the `.clj`\n  is the file that would load.\n- `core_test` declares 35 `deftest` in the `.clj` and 73 in the `.cljc`, and\n  **every one of the 35 names also appears in the `.cljc`** \u2014 zero names exist\n  only in the `.clj`. The shadowed file is a strict superset.\n- `comfy_test` is the same shape, 6 against 7.\n\nSo 39 tests are written in files the JVM would not load. That is the finding\nthe sweep was pointing at.\n\n**And it is currently moot, which is the part worth writing down.**\n`clojure -M:test` in that repository does not build a classpath at all: it\nexits 1 with *Unable to compare versions for io.github.kotoba-lang/sekisho*,\nwhich is declared somewhere in its transitive graph both as a `:git/sha` and as\na `:local/root`. tools.deps cannot reconcile those, so **no test in that\nrepository runs, shadowed or not.**\n\nTwo lessons, and the second is the one that generalises. First: examining a\npair can produce a real finding, so *unexamined* is worth converting rather\nthan leaving. Second: the examination found a larger fault than the one it went\nlooking for, and had it stopped at the twin it would have reported *39 tests do\nnot run* while the true figure is *none of them do*. A shadowed file is only\nthe second thing wrong when the suite cannot start.\n\nNeither is fixed here. Both are in another component, and the dependency\nconflict was not traced to the declaration that causes it \u2014 a repository-wide\nsearch for it did not finish on a machine at load average 145, and guessing at\na transitive pin is not a fix.")))
